    "result_text": "Full build passes. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the GOLD tier rule with a single-file change to `RespondWithOwner.java` (the GET responder that builds the owner DTO).\n\n**Change** (`rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwner.java`): after the mapper produces the DTO, count the current household members (owners resolving to the same `HouseholdId` \u2014 same normalized lastName + address) and override the tier to `\"GOLD\"` when there are 3 or more:\n\n```java\nString householdId = HouseholdId.of(owner.getLastName(), owner.getAddress());\nlong members = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n        .filter(o -> householdId.equals(HouseholdId.of(o.getLastName(), o.getAddress())))\n        .count();\nif (members >= 3) {\n    dto.setMembershipTier(\"GOLD\");\n}\n```\n\n**Why this shape (low structural impact):**\n- `RespondWithOwner` already had the `OwnerRepository` in hand and already post-processed the DTO (`setBulkSignupWarning`), so the household lookup slots in with no new dependencies.\n- It reuses the existing `HouseholdId.of(...)` helper \u2014 the same normalization `RequireUniqueHousehold` uses \u2014 so membership is computed consistently with no duplicated matching logic.\n- BRONZE/SILVER are untouched: the mapper still derives them; GOLD is layered as an upgrade only when the threshold is met.\n- **One file, one method, no new class, no YAML, no DB column.** I avoided persisting a household-count field (which would have dragged in `Owner.java`, `schema.sql`, the mapper, a new step class, and the POST YAML), keeping the file-count multiplier at 1 and the changed function simple.\n\nTests: the new `AcceptanceTest.coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold` passes and the full suite is green.",
